<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narshakhi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">899–959</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Abu Bakr Narshakhi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narshakhi (Abu Bakr Muhammad Narshakhi) was a historian from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> oasis who in 943 or 944 wrote a History of Bukhara in Arabic for the Samanid ruler Nuh I. The work survives only in a shortened Persian translation of 1128 with later additions. It is the main source for the pre-Islamic and early Islamic history of Bukhara, including its local rulers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhar-khudah">Bukhar Khudahs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narshakhi.jpg" alt="Narshakhi" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ernest Leroux, 1892 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DeBoukhara_Cover.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Jafar Narshakhi was a Sogdian from the village of Narshak in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> oasis, and he is the first known historian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. Nothing is known of him beyond his authorship of this one book. In his day Persian had largely replaced Sogdian in the populous parts of Sogdia, but he probably still spoke Sogdian well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>He wrote the History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> (Tarikh-i Bukhara) in a time of upheaval for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>. Nasr II had converted to Isma'ili Shi'ism, which had spread through the work of missionaries and was regarded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> as a threat. His son Nuh faced turmoil in which his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and other members of the court were killed, and he purged the missionaries and killed Isma'ili converts, though the court remained tense. The book was composed for a patron who supported Sunni Islam against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Isma'ilism</a>, and the Samanids took the role of guardians of Sunni law.</p><p>The book preserves information about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> found in no other contemporary source. Abu Nasr Ahmad al-Qubavi's Persian translation, made in the 12th century, both shortened the text and added material extending the history to 975. This Persian version was in turn abridged by Muhammad ibn Zufar ibn 'Umar later in the 12th century. Charles-Henri-Auguste Schefer published an abridged French translation in 1892, and in 1954 the historian Richard N. Frye translated the Persian abridgment into English.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Narshakhi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tradition, recorded by <b>Narshakhi</b>, links its foundation to settlers and princes from the Hephthalite and Turkic period.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bukhara">Principality of Bukhara</a></li></ul></section>
